#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064169 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064040 :question/id #uuid "c25e6037-3900-5314-b9a9-262fef07061c" :question/math-academy-id "e-368" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\dfrac 1 {x^3}$ and that $F'(x) = f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if $F'(x) = f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac 1 {x^3}= x^{-3}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-3$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3+1}}{-3+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{-2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3174cae9-484e-5ca3-8e5c-3c9cddc86145" :knowledge-point/level-coverage [{:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186725828 :question-generator/id #uuid "79251234-317f-5359-8cd5-5b0af0564553" :question-generator/script "generators/71/7104e60aefdf636e72a65afc605c675701814c9a1c111019c7aa27da23d4c6e8/generator.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064722 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6a907ad-e7e6-5e6b-abb5-c41f16606605" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2/3} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062390} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6a8d1e-0787-5cc4-88df-ddf958e1c1d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x^{-1/3} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062391} {:answer/id #uuid "16cba4e2-5699-54d0-b0af-01f1431acd0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}x^{5/3} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062392} {:answer/id #uuid "1b2e4389-0b1c-5925-9f9f-a24ca4b82e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^{1/3} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062393} {:answer/id #uuid "70c4a8d8-2ed6-56b1-a746-7e1fc22b8346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}x^{-1/3} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b2e4389-0b1c-5925-9f9f-a24ca4b82e65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x^{1/3} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062393} :answer-field/id #uuid "29757ce8-69c0-518a-9094-1478bb25152b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062389}] :question/id #uuid "35560944-ec26-532a-8ab4-c27135900127" :question/math-academy-id "q-29987" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = x^{-2/3}$ and that $F^{′}(x) = f(x)$, calculate $F(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{2}{3}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=3x^{1/3} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(3 \\sqrt[3]{x}+C\\right)=\\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{2}{3}}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064723 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af3857a5-22ad-5617-8dd5-fe36c075509e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}x^{3/2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062396} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2aaad4-968f-574b-842f-23871805d75b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x^{2/5} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062397} {:answer/id #uuid "35a1e4d4-ceef-5bb3-8381-e67a3cd0b1b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}x^{5/2} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062398} {:answer/id #uuid "40c5bb75-6c13-5d38-b5c4-d450f209706c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}x^{2/5} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062399} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb175d1-16eb-5478-ba97-5cb4d5899d6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}x^{2/5} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5eb175d1-16eb-5478-ba97-5cb4d5899d6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}x^{2/5} + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062400} :answer-field/id #uuid "4af9582e-4d65-5832-94c1-0ccba896c739" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186062395}] :question/id #uuid "4f854a01-0859-5bcc-add9-c9a801f5da45" :question/math-academy-id "q-29989" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\frac{1}{x^{3/5}}$ and that $F^{′}(x) = f(x)$, calculate $F(x)$." :question/worked-solution "Apply the sum and constant multiple rules, using the power rule or the standard trigonometric/exponential antiderivative for each term:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{3}{5}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\frac{5}{2}x^{2/5} + C.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the result to check:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5 x^{\\frac{2}{5}}}{2}+C\\right)=\\frac{1}{x^{\\frac{3}{5}}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233179 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "25edeff2-faa4-50c4-bbfd-3291d7ac7694" :question/math-academy-id "q-113705" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4/3}}$ and that ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{'}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{'}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{'}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4/3}}={x}^{-4/3}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{4}{3}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-4/3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4/3+1}}{(-\\frac{4}{3}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/3}}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =-3{x}^{-1/3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{{x}^{1/3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233751 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a192223-274d-5d58-847a-c92d2faa98df" :question/math-academy-id "q-29633" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Note that $\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}={x}^{-4}.$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-4$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{-4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-4+1}}{-4+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{{x}^{-3}}{3}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3{x}^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233752 :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8dcd24a2-ce5f-5ab6-8a92-814e8f1355a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-29662" :question/problem "Calculate the integral of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}$" :question/worked-solution "Note that $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{5/2}}={x}^{-5/2}.\n$$ So, we can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}} & =\\int {x}^{-5/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-5/2+1}}{-\\frac{5}{2}+1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{-3/2}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3/2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234299 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21fbeb51-6dc3-543d-a41d-b167ecce3c56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}{x}^{-6/7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513623} {:answer/id #uuid "3a50a15f-c07f-5fc0-8f8a-c9858dca85ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513684} {:answer/id #uuid "47d643d4-e671-5e72-9cf0-0a89cbdb8d8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answ